gluon> :since 1.27 -lang
!view
!clip 24
go 1.27 · generic methods

A method may declare its own type parameters, so a type can carry a generic
operation in its own namespace rather than forcing it out into a package-level
function. Box[int].Map to a Box[string] is the example everyone reaches for, and
until now it could not be a method at all: the receiver's type parameters were
the only ones a method was allowed, so Map had to become func Map[T, U any](Box[T], func(T) U) Box[U]
and read backwards.

The restriction was in the original generics design and was expected to be
temporary. What it cost was not expressiveness — the free function always
worked — but the ability to write a fluent chain, and to keep an operation on
the type it belongs to.

Needs go 1.27 — under a lower go directive the compiler rejects it.
Proposal: https://go.dev/issue/77273

type Box[T any] struct{ V T }
func (b Box[T]) Map[U any](f func(T) U) Box[U] {
	return Box[U]{V: f(b.V)}
}
Box[int]{V: 3}.Map(func(n int) bool { return n > 2 })

────────

go 1.27 · a struct literal key may be any field selector

A key in a struct literal may be any valid field selector for the type, not only
a field declared directly on it. A field promoted from an embedded struct can be
set in the literal, which is how it could already be read and assigned — so the
composite literal stops being the one place embedding does not apply.

Before this, a literal had to name the embedded type and nest a second literal
inside it, which meant the shape of your literals depended on which fields
happened to be embedded rather than on what you were setting.

Needs go 1.27 — under a lower go directive the compiler rejects it.
Proposal: https://go.dev/issue/9859

type Base struct{ ID int }
type User struct {
	Base
	Name string
}
u := User{ID: 7, Name: "ada"}
[]any{u.ID, u.Name}

────────

go 1.27 · type inference when a generic function is assigned or converted

Function type inference now applies everywhere a generic function is assigned to
a variable of a matching function type, or converted to one. Previously the
contexts it applied in were enumerated, and a conversion was not among them, so
BinOp(Pick) had to be written BinOp(Pick[int]) — spelling out an argument the
target type already determined.

Like Go 1.26's self-referential constraints, no go directive gates this: it is a
relaxation of what the type checker will infer, so it works under any go line on
a new enough toolchain, and there is no version below which it fails.

Needs go 1.27 — no go directive gates it; only the toolchain decides.
Proposal: https://go.dev/issue/77245

func Pick[T any](a, b T) T { return a }
type BinOp func(int, int) int
f := BinOp(Pick)
f(3, 9)
